  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 PM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThis afternoon, RAP surface analysis shows a weak shortwave embedded \nwithin departing ridging lifting through the area. GOES water vapor \nimagery shows ample moisture at all atmospheric levels, while \ntemperatures rise well above normal despite widespread cloud cover. \nMost of the UP is realizing temperatures approaching 20F above \nnormal, as temperatures currently sit in the high-50s and low-60s, \nwith some spots achieving mid-60s. Light, southwesterly flow is \nobserved at the surface while 500 mb flow is slowly backing from \nwesterly to south-southwesterly. \n\nMeanwhile, a robust longwave trough digs into southern California. \nAs this trough progresses east through the week, the UP ends up in a \nrelatively long-lived WAA regime with connection to Gulf moisture, \nresulting in above-normal temperatures and high chances of a wetting \nrainfall. In the lee of the Rockies, a closed Colorado low \nassociated with this trough forms Monday evening, deepening quickly \nto 995mb and lifting northeast into the Northern Plains. Its \nprogression stalls throughout Tuesday as the low weakens slightly \nand dawdles for over 24 hours, reaching MN's Arrowhead only by early \nThursday morning. While agreement exists that this low will slow \ndown in the Upper Midwest for a time mid-week, the exact placement \nand progression of the low look to be a toss-up, according to \ncluster analysis and run-to-run consistency. Thus, QPF amounts and \ntiming will continue to evolve with this forecast, including late-\nweek continued precipitation.\n\nAt this time, the bulk of this early week's precipitation looks to \ncommence Monday evening, with most of the UP seeing 50-80% chance of \ngreater than 1\" of precipitation falling between Monday at 7pm (EST) \nand Tuesday at 7pm (EST). Rain will linger into Wednesday; 48-hour \nprobabilities of greater than 2\" range from 10-30% for a period \nending 7pm (EST) Wednesday. WPC has the UP in a Day 2 Marginal Risk \nfor excessive rainfall, aligning with the period of heaviest \nanticipated rainfall during the first half of Tuesday. Flooding \nconcerns are not anticipated at this time, given the ongoing \ndrought. Local observations compiled by the Midwestern Regional \nClimate Center show nearly all of the UP as receiving between 3\" and \n6\" less than normal between Sept 1 and Oct 31; for the NWS office in \nNegaunee, this number is 3.73\".\n\nFor temperatures, highs in the 60s are expected through Tuesday \nalong with lows in the low 50s on Monday and Tuesday nights (high \n40s for tonight, Sunday). Highs start to trend down on Wednesday, \nbut remain anomalously elevated until Friday, when the low passes to \nour east and CAA takes over temporarily. At the most, these early-\nweek high temperatures represent 20-25F from normal (Tuesday), and \ndepartures of 25-30F for low temperatures (Monday and Tuesday \nnights). Despite some cooler air on Friday, this coming weekend \nsuggests continued warm temperatures, and CPC outlooks place Upper \nMichigan at 60-70% chance of above-normal temps for Nov 8-14. Thus, \nwe can expect the general trend of (relatively) warm weather to \ncontinue into November.",
